{"answerText":"No. A gas appliance is not categorically prohibited in every bathroom, but the baseline rule prohibits locating appliances in bathrooms. The listed exceptions permit certain installations in rooms not used for sleeping: (1) a direct-vent appliance that obtains all combustion air directly from outdoors and is installed under its listing and manufacturer instructions; (2) specified vented heaters, fireplaces, and decorative appliances where the room meets the Section 304.5 volume criteria; or (3) an appliance in an enclosure taking all combustion air from outdoors, with access through a solid, weather-stripped, approved self-closing door. (FGC § 303.3.)

The special provisions in FGC § 303.3.1 apply to gas-fired direct-vent **space-heating** appliances used to heat sleeping rooms—not bathrooms—and deem a compliant appliance to be outside the sleeping room. They therefore do not create a separate bathroom exception. If that special sleeping-room provision is being used, the appliance must, among other requirements, be installed by a master licensed plumber; the supplied text also requires additional CO detectors associated with that appliance, not interconnected with other dwelling-unit CO detectors. (FGC §§ 303.3.1, 303.3.1.6.)

For the proposed bathroom installation, compliance depends on the actual appliance type and which § 303.3 exception is claimed. Material verification includes the appliance listing and manufacturer installation instructions; for exception 1, confirmation that all combustion air is directly from outdoors; for exception 2, the applicable Section 304.5 volume criteria; or, for exception 3, the enclosure combustion-air arrangement and specified door features. The supplied evidence does not establish those project-specific conditions or provide the Section 304.5 or 304.6 requirements.","supportedPoints":[{"heading":"Bathroom location is prohibited by default, subject to stated exceptions","explanation":"FGC § 303.3 prohibits locating appliances in bathrooms. It then provides three exceptions for rooms other than sleeping rooms: qualifying direct-vent appliances using all outdoor combustion air and installed per listing and manufacturer instructions; specified vented heating, fireplace, and decorative appliances in rooms meeting Section 304.5 volume criteria; and appliances in an enclosure using all outdoor combustion air with the prescribed solid, weather-stripped, approved self-closing access door.","sectionID":"8063","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:8063-1"]},{"heading":"Special direct-vent rule addresses sleeping rooms","explanation":"FGC § 303.3.1 deems qualifying gas-fired direct-vent space-heating appliances used to heat sleeping rooms to be outside the sleeping room, if the appliance and installation comply with Sections 303.3.1.1 through 303.3.1.7. Its stated scope does not establish a separate bathroom exception.","sectionID":"8064","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:8064-2"]},{"heading":"Installation conditions for the special sleeping-room direct-vent provision","explanation":"For gas-fired direct-vent space-heating appliances within the § 303.3.1 pathway, FGC § 303.3.1.6 requires additional CO detectors with the appliance, prohibits their interconnection to other dwelling-unit CO detection devices, and requires installation by a master licensed plumber.","sectionID":"8070","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:8070-3"]}],"assumptions":[],"missingFacts":["The proposed appliance type and whether it is direct-vent, one of the specifically listed vented appliance types, or proposed within a separate enclosure.","The appliance listing and manufacturer installation instructions.","If relying on FGC § 303.3 exception 1, whether all combustion air is obtained directly from outdoors.","If relying on FGC § 303.3 exception 2, the room volume and the applicable FGC § 304.5 volume criteria.","If relying on FGC § 303.3 exception 3, whether all combustion air for the enclosure is taken from outdoors and whether enclosure access is through a solid, weather-stripped door with an approved self-closing device."],"followUpQuestions":[],"evidenceLimitations":["Permitext limited this answer to the enacted evidence assembled for the current question.","The supplied evidence does not include the text of FGC §§ 304.5 or 304.6, so it cannot determine whether a proposed bathroom meets the referenced volume or enclosure-combustion-air conditions."],"additionalEvidenceNeeded":["The appliance cut sheet/listing and manufacturer installation instructions.","If exception 2 is claimed, the applicable text of FGC § 304.5 and the bathroom volume.","If exception 3 is claimed, the applicable text of FGC § 304.6 and enclosure/door details."],"supportingSourceUses":[],"citations":[{"sectionID":"8063","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:8063-1"],"relevance":"Establishes the baseline bathroom prohibition and its three express exceptions."},{"sectionID":"8064","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:8064-2"],"relevance":"Establishes the limited sleeping-room scope of the special direct-vent provision."},{"sectionID":"8070","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:8070-3"],"relevance":"Establishes installation conditions under the special sleeping-room direct-vent provision."}]}

{"answerText":"**No—not for the stated tables-and-chairs use.** A 900-net-square-foot room used as unconcentrated assembly space with tables and chairs has an occupant load of **60 persons** (900 ÷ 15 net square feet/person). A room used for assembly purposes with an occupant load of fewer than 75 persons is classified as Group B or, when accessory to another occupancy, as part of that occupancy—not Group A-3. (BC §§ 303.1.2, 303.1.3, Table 1004.1.3.)

The residents-only limitation does not by itself establish the legal accessory relationship, but it does not change this result for the stated 60-person use: both the accessory-space and nonaccessory-tenant-space provisions classify sub-75 assembly space as Group B (or, for an accessory room, part of the principal occupancy). Group A-3 otherwise includes recreation, amusement, and other assembly uses not classified elsewhere in Group A. (BC §§ 303.1.2, 303.1.3, 303.4.)

This conclusion is limited to the stated tables-and-chairs function. Because the room is multipurpose, it must comply with requirements applicable to every purpose for which it will be occupied. A different intended setup can change the occupant load—for example, the table lists 7 net square feet per occupant for concentrated movable-chair seating—and could exceed 74 persons in the same 900-net-square-foot room. (BC § 302.1; Table 1004.1.3.)","supportedPoints":[{"heading":"Sub-75 assembly spaces are not Group A-3","explanation":"An assembly-purpose room or space with an occupant load of fewer than 75 persons and accessory to another occupancy must be classified as Group B or as part of that occupancy. A building or nonaccessory tenant space used for assembly purposes with an occupant load below 75 persons must be classified as Group B. The accessory provision preserves an option to calculate that room’s plumbing fixtures using assembly-occupancy requirements.","sectionID":"132","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:132-4","research-permitext_discovered-id:131-5"]},{"heading":"Tables-and-chairs occupant-load calculation","explanation":"Table 1004.1.3 assigns unconcentrated assembly space with tables and chairs an occupant-load factor of 15 net square feet per occupant. At 900 net square feet, that produces an occupant load of 60 persons.","sectionID":"2152","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:2152-2"]},{"heading":"Multipurpose use must be evaluated for each intended purpose","explanation":"A room or space intended for occupation at different times for different purposes must comply with all requirements applicable to each intended purpose. The same table assigns different factors to other assembly arrangements, including 7 net square feet per occupant for concentrated movable-chair seating.","sectionID":"128","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:128-3","research-permitext_discovered-id:2152-2"]},{"heading":"Scope of Group A-3","explanation":"Group A-3 includes assembly uses intended for worship, recreation, amusement, and other assembly uses not classified elsewhere in Group A, including the listed examples. The sub-75 assembly-space provisions establish the applicable Group B or principal-occupancy classification path for the stated 60-person use.","sectionID":"135","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:135-1","research-permitext_discovered-id:132-4","research-permitext_discovered-id:131-5"]}],"assumptions":[],"missingFacts":["The other intended room functions and corresponding layouts or occupant loads, if the room will be used for activities beyond the stated normal tables-and-chairs arrangement. Those uses could produce an occupant load of 75 or more.","Whether the room is legally accessory to the apartment building’s principal occupancy. This affects whether it is classified as part of that occupancy or as Group B, but does not change the stated 60-person result from being outside Group A-3."],"followUpQuestions":[],"evidenceLimitations":["The supplied enacted evidence establishes the relevant occupancy-classification threshold and occupant-load factors, but does not establish every intended multipurpose-room activity or layout.","This answer is limited to the 2022 NYC Construction Codes enacted passages supplied for the question."],"additionalEvidenceNeeded":["The planned alternate functions, furniture layouts, and calculated occupant load for each intended use, if any use beyond unconcentrated tables and chairs is proposed.","Approved occupancy records or plans showing the room’s relationship to the principal apartment-building occupancy, if the distinction between Group B and part of the principal occupancy must be documented."],"supportingSourceUses":[],"citations":[{"sectionID":"2152","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:2152-2"],"relevance":"Establishes the 15-net-square-foot-per-occupant factor for unconcentrated assembly space with tables and chairs, as well as the alternative assembly factors."},{"sectionID":"132","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:132-4"],"relevance":"Establishes the classification rule for an accessory assembly room with fewer than 75 occupants and the plumbing-fixture calculation permission."},{"sectionID":"131","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:131-5"],"relevance":"Establishes the Group B classification rule for a nonaccessory tenant assembly space with fewer than 75 occupants."},{"sectionID":"128","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:128-3"],"relevance":"Establishes that a room used at different times for different purposes must comply with requirements applicable to every intended purpose."},{"sectionID":"135","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:135-1"],"relevance":"Establishes the stated scope of Group A-3 assembly uses."}]}
